¶
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' To ''Abolish, Repeal, Rescind, Recall, Revoke, Abrogate, Annul, Cancel,'' end, destroy, do away with, set aside, nullify, annihilate, quash, vacate, make void, extirpate, eradicate, suppress, uproot, erase, expunge. ''Abolish'' is a strong word, and signifies a complete removal, generally but not always by a summary act. It is the word specially used in connection with things that have been long established or deeply rooted, as an institution or a custom: as, to ''abolish'' slavery or polygamy. ''Repeal'' is generally used of the formal rescinding of a legislative act. ''Abrogate,'' to abolish summarily, more often as the act of a ruler, but sometimes of a representative body. ''Annul,'' literally to bring to nothing, to deprive of all force or obligation, as a law or contract. ''Rescind'' (literally, to cut short) is coextensive in meaning with both ''repeal'' and ''annul. Recall, revoke'' (see ''renounce''). ''Cancel'' is not used of laws, but of deeds, bonds, contracts, etc., and figuratively of whatever may be thought of as crossed out. [In legal parlance, ''rescind'' is never applied to a statute; it is the common expression for the act of a party in justly repudiating a contract. ''Repeal'' is never applied to a contract; it is the common expression for the termination of the existence of a statute by a later statute. ''Annul'' is the common expression for the judicial act of a court in terminating the existence of any obligation or conveyance. ''Cancel'' is used when the instrument is obliterated actually or in legal contemplation; the other words when the obligation is annihilated irrespective of whether the instrument is left intact or not.]}}
{{smaller block|I have never doubted the constitutional authority of Congress to ''abolish'' slavery in this District [of Columbia].
{{right|''Lincoln'', in Raymond, p. 184.}}}}
{{smaller block|Leaving out amended acts and enumerating only acts entirely ''repealed'', the result is that in the last three sessions there have been ''repealed'' . . . 650 acts belonging to the present reign. {{right|''H. Spencer'', Pop. Sci. Mo., XXV. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|The king also ''rescinded'' the order by which the Bishop of London had been suspended from the exercise of his functions.
{{right|''Buckle.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Whose laws, like those of the Medes and Persians, they cannot alter or ''abrogate''.
{{right|''Burke.''}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|Your promises are sins of inconsideration at best; and you are bound to repent and ''annul'' them.
{{right|''Swift.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I here forget all former griefs,
''Cancel'' all grudge.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., v. 4.}}}}

'''abolishable''' (a̤-bol′ish-a̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''abolish'' + ''-able.'' Cf. F. ''abolissable.''] Capable of being abolished or annulled, as a law, rite, custom, etc.; that may be set aside or destroyed.
{{smaller block|And yet . . . hope is but deferred; not abolished, not ''abolishable.''
{{right|''Carlyle'', French Rev., I. ii. 8.}}}}

'''abolisher''' (a̤-bol′ish-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''abolish'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which abolishes.

'''abolishment''' (a̤-bol′ish-ment), ''n.'' [< ''abolish'' + ''-ment.'' Cf. F. ''abolissement.''] The act of abolishing or of putting an end to; abrogation; destruction; abolition. [Now rare.]
{{smaller block|He should think the ''abolishment'' of Episcopacy among us would prove a mighty scandal.
{{right|''Swift'', Sent. of a Ch. of Eng. Man.}}}}

'''abolition''' (ab-ō-lish′o̤n), ''n.'' [< F. ''abolition'', < L. ''abolitio(n-)'', < ''abolēre'', annul, abolish: see ''abolish.''] '''1.''' The act of abolishing, or the state of being abolished; annulment; abrogation; utter destruction: as, the ''abolition'' of laws, decrees, ordinances, rites, customs, debts, etc.; the ''abolition'' of slavery. {{smaller|The most frequent use of the word in recent times has been in connection with the effort to put an end to the system of slavery, which was finally accomplished in the United States in 1865 by the thirteenth amendment to the Constitution.}}
{{smaller block|For the amalgamation of races, and for the ''abolition'' of villenage, she [Britain] is chiefly indebted to the influence which the priesthood in the middle ages exercised over the laity.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., i.}}}}

